WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.480
These are the faces of women

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.170
who have come to represent progress.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.810
Rwanda has led the world

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.120
in the levels of women in its parliament,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.770
now for over a decade.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.010
Nearly two thirds of Rwanda's parliament

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.600
is made up of women.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.748
That's more than twice the global average.

00:00:28.748 --> 00:00:31.320
Countries that have more women in power

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.170
tend to have a better
outcomes for development.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.020
So, better outcomes for child mortality,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.640
better outcomes for education.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.620
Countries that have higher levels of women

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.770
in their government and also just general

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.150
better levels of gender
equality, more broadly

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:53.150
are more likely to be stable and secure.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.730
But the origins of Rwanda's
high female representation

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.735
stem from the country's darkest moment.

00:01:00.735 --> 00:01:03.030
Women must know that things have changed.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.610
We can do, we can equally
do what men can do.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.090
The whole world now wants
to learn from Rwanda

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.120
about gender, about women in Rwanda.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.650
How are they developing?

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.010
What are they achieving?

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.410
While Rwanda leads the world
in female representation,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.940
the motivations of the ruling
party have raised questions

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.475
about the true extent of women's power.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.500
In April 1994, Rwandan
President Juvénal Habyarimana

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.420
was killed when his plane was shot down

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.363
near the country's capital, Kigali.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.950
The assassination ignited ethnic tensions

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.700
between the majority, Hutu and
the minority, Tutsi tribes.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.890
In 100 days of hatred, 800,000 died.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:59.290
The violence would leave a
10th of the population dead.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.460
While the conflict was
undoubtedly framed by ethnicity,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.920
its effect on women was undeniable.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.970
The genocide was planned,

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.840
not just killing but other things,

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.050
rape was a reward the leaders
gave to those who killed.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.220
Most women had been gang raped.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.075
That was very traumatic.

00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:23.621
Some of them are mutilated.

00:02:23.621 --> 00:02:25.200
Some of them have had children

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.750
born out of the rape.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.250
Women were targeted
because of who they were.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:35.250
And it shocked the world to
see that even your own husband,

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.120
whom you have given beautiful children

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.710
comes around and slaughters you

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.810
because you belong to that ethnic group.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.750
Many others were, you know, disconnected

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.830
and kind of separated from their children,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.860
from their families.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.390
In spite of this horror,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.830
it was women who would
rebuild the country.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.810
It is now among the most
stable countries in Africa,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.785
average incomes have tripled.

00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:00.522
Educational, Healthcare, Tourism

00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:03.510
and Trade are all improving dramatically.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.550
Rwandan leader Paul Kagame

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.040
has been assailed by
international observers

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.730
for suppressing political
opponents and dissent,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.930
sometimes violently.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.810
But he and his ruling party, the RPF,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.760
have also been praised for
stabilizing the country

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.430
and setting it on a course of recovery.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.980
Part of this redevelopment
was enabling Rwandan women,

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.280
who had found themselves
taking on new roles

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.580
that had traditionally belonged to men.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.940
Burying the dead, building homes,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.720
as well as housing half a million orphans.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.010
I'm always thinking of stories

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.950
that women have told me over the years

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.080
about how before the genocide,
they were a housewife

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.230
and they were living sort of,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.400
you know, kind of day to
day, raising children,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.580
cooking, caring for their plot of land

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.390
and how in the aftermath of the violence,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.810
they found themselves alone

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.810
and completely realizing
that they needed to act,

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.080
they needed to pull people together.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.040
They needed to do something
to be able to survive

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.450
the utterly abysmal and
horrible conditions of life.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.010
Especially in rural communities

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.490
in the aftermath of the bloodshed.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.550
Land has gone fallow,
massive displacement.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.560
I mean, about 50% of the population

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.110
was displaced from their homes.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.080
And so you see a total of
erosion of social networks.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.130
You know, you no longer have
your community to be with.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.000
You have to forge new networks

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.958
and forge new, you know,

00:04:33.958 --> 00:04:37.716
ways of providing for yourself

00:04:37.716 --> 00:04:40.735
and for your children and
for others in your community.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.790
Just a few years after the violence,

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.890
women had formed an estimated 15,400

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.350
organizations across the country.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.160
Initially, with a focus on
grief, trauma and support.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.830
Mary Balikungeri founded
the Rwanda Women's Network.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.860
In our safe space you could meet and cry,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.473
talk about our challenges,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.110
talk about issues that
really concerned us.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.636
But at the same time, ask
ourselves, what should we do?

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.100
So out of that safe space,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.540
women have learnt to plan, to organize,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.840
to take awareness to their fellow women

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.300
and eventually to connect
to the whole thinking

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.553
to the whole political
thinking of the country.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:41.090
And in that journey of
safety has given us the power

00:05:41.092 --> 00:05:44.243
to know that you can change a society.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.560
Some of these organizations

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.520
would evolve into vocational training

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.550
and profit-making businesses.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.380
By joining together and
formalizing their structure,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.030
groups could become eligible
for economic benefits

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.450
from NGOs or the government.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.080
In the process of formalizing,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.370
they established a
president, a vice president,

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.840
a secretary, a treasurer

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.620
and this provided really
a kind of a platform

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.640
for women's leadership
within these really nascent,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.720
small, community organizations,
that then women could use

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.253
to kind of support their

00:06:18.253 --> 00:06:22.370
increased engagement in politics.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:24.460
This increasing engagement of women

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.620
in what had been a typical patriarchy,

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.970
would have a broader social impact.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.290
From the clothes they wore,

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.480
to the businesses they started to run,

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.770
ideas of what women should
be were about to change.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:39.050
When you look back
before the colonial era,

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.050
definitely women were, you
know, were very active,

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.970
active but yet invisible

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:51.340
and that's the typical patriarchal society

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:54.270
that most African countries are.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:59.240
Patriarchy is a very hard system to undo.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.960
It takes courage, it takes time,

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.990
because it is culturally grounded.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:09.800
To begin undoing the system

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.980
there was one aspect of Rwandan society

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.730
that needed to change
quickly: land rights.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.940
With more than 90% of Rwandans

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.480
dependent on agriculture
for their livelihood,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.210
access was vital in the recovery.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.500
Women's organizations mobilized
to take control of land

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.820
owned by deceased family members.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.020
In 1999, the Succession Law was passed,

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.660
granting women the right to
inherit land and property

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.460
for the first time.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.260
This would set the stage
for subsequent legislation

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.050
that established children's rights

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.817
and protection against
gender-based violence.

00:07:44.817 --> 00:07:47.866
At the time they inheritance
laws were not there,

00:07:47.866 --> 00:07:50.917
and so we had to fight for
it in order to make sure

00:07:50.917 --> 00:07:54.720
that women are able to inherit
their husband's properties.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.010
We want to be able to forcefully

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.910
put forward the women's needs,

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.210
because women's needs are not men's needs

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.210
and nobody will talk about women's needs

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.228
better than you as a woman.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.310
With just 20 judges left
alive after the genocide,

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:18.810
more women also became
involved in Gacaca courts,

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.900
village-based justice proceedings

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.920
designed to try hundreds of thousands

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.040
accused of crimes during the genocide.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.890
By 2001, about 35% of newly-elected

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:31.763
Gacaca judges were women.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.540
Then in August, 2003, a new
constitution was written,

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.610
as well as committing to
fight the ideology of genocide

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.650
and eradicate ethnic divisions,

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:48.210
the constitution also
pursues gender equality.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.800
It states that women are
granted at least 30% of posts

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:54.210
in decision making organs.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:57.310
Research shows that 30%
is the critical mass

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.660
of women in power needed

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.140
to make a substantial
difference in politics.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.810
In Rwanda, those seats
are elected by voters

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.660
from local women's councils,

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.970
but women are also able to contest seats

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.350
outside of this allocation.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.690
In the first election after the genocide,

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:17.281
women won 48% of seats.

00:09:17.281 --> 00:09:21.990
This really was, I think, evidence

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.360
of a broader swell of support,

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.290
both kind of from the political leadership

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.290
as well as from the grassroots,

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:32.290
for women's political participation

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.330
in this sort of new rebuilding Rwanda.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:39.680
Those sorts of informal,
grassroots, self-help projects

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.480
became the foundation for building

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.833
a more engaged kind of political,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.741
kind of population of women.

00:09:48.741 --> 00:09:50.280
The narrative has changed,

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.190
before it was only a male-dominated space,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.721
but now we are together in it.

00:09:55.721 --> 00:10:00.721
So the more we continue to
engage ourselves with them

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:05.670
and the more we continue to reason them,

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:09.360
and that's why we said
the narrative is changing,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.826
and it has changed for good.

00:10:11.826 --> 00:10:14.220
By the next election, in 2013,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.930
the number of women elected rose to 64%.

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:20.490
Led by Paul Kagame, the ruling party,

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.470
the Rwandan Patriotic Front

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.320
has made women's inclusion
a hallmark of its program

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.600
for post-genocide recovery
and reconstruction.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.780
But despite the significant
steps in female empowerment

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.400
and broader development
following the genocide,

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.760
human rights advocates are highly critical

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.290
of the way in which Kagame's
party runs the country.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.290
Freedom House ranks Rwanda as not free,

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.960
with concern about rights and liberties.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.760
Human Rights Watch says the government

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.410
displays a marked intolerance

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.330
of the most basic forms of dissent.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:53.840
Amnesty's International Rights Reports

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.010
in the lead up to the 2017 election

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.310
that talks about opposition politicians,

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.160
journalists, human rights
defenders being jailed,

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.500
physically attacked, even killed.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.808
I think it's rubbish, absolute rubbish.

00:11:03.808 --> 00:11:04.641
You reject that?

00:11:04.641 --> 00:11:05.913
Rwanda is completely different

00:11:05.913 --> 00:11:08.800
from what it was 25 years ago.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.070
And although the RPF
welcomed women into politics,

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.890
critics suggest the motives
aren't as noble as once thought.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:19.090
Women who were seen as less
complicit in the genocide

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.440
could be used to the party's advantage.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:26.050
Many of the boldest and
most kind of fierce leaders

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:29.740
within civil society were
brought into government,

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.450
so that they could be, I think, greater

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:37.350
kind of policed in a way that is really,

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:40.380
has been detrimental
for the kind of growth

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.717
of democracy in Rwanda.

00:11:43.717 --> 00:11:46.300
I think the notable thing
about the women seats,

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.260
is that they are not affiliated
with a political party,

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:50.960
so they are just women's seats.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.500
And so critics have really suggested

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.670
that this has allowed
Rwandan Patriotic Front

00:11:56.670 --> 00:12:00.050
to cement an even
greater political control

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:01.310
over the parliament.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:04.310
Because while the number of
seats held by RPF members

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:08.160
is whatever it is, the number
of seats that are held by RPF

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.240
kind of supporters

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.860
and, you know, those closely
affiliated with the party

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.610
are often on the women's tickets

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:17.900
and so they're not actually,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.340
they're not actually recorded

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.450
as being a member of a political party

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.050
while they're in office.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.240
When we look at the cases of Diane Rwigara

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.140
or Victoire Ingabire, these are two women

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.990
that have dared to challenge

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.980
President Kagame for the presidency,

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:36.240
and both were met with extreme sanctions.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:37.860
They were thrown in jail.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.730
I think those cases
are really illustrative

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.330
in showing the limits

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.410
of the story of women's
empowerment in Rwanda.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.250
We do see these kind of amazing levels

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:50.980
of women in government,
but at the same time,

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:54.510
what, you know, what power
does a parliament have

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:58.583
if they are completely under
the control of the Executive?

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:18.650
Women have power to the extent

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:21.380
that what they're
interested in and working on

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.980
does not conflict with
the goals of the regime.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:28.230
So there have been some kind of notable,

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:31.290
I think, pieces of legislation,

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.620
especially around gender-based violence

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.350
that women parliamentarians
in Rwanda have pushed forward

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.020
and which have made important,

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.933
impressive sort of legislative
change in the country.

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.710
Despite this legislation,

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:53.650
levels of violence
against women remain high.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:57.850
Estimates in 2010, found 41%
of women have experienced

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.360
some form of physical
violence since the age of 15,

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.216
rising to 56% for married women.

00:14:05.216 --> 00:14:09.660
We see really the challenge

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:13.870
in assuming that women's
political empowerment

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.670
translates or trickles down

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:18.460
to women's empowerment, writ large, right?

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.630
We see remarkably high rates
of gender-based violence,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.440
of intimate partner violence.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:25.140
We see remarkably high rates

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.593
of women's economic disenfranchisement.

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:32.860
The story on Rwanda tends
to be quite polarized.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:37.660
Just 25 years after
absolutely catastrophic

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:40.750
and horrific violence,

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:44.130
you see a country that hasn't
seen a return to bloodshed.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.500
You see a country where there have been

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:51.326
profound increases in
longevity, in Health,

00:14:51.326 --> 00:14:55.890
in Education, you know,
and that story is one

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.660
that really should be
celebrated and commended.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.120
And then on the other hand,

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.080
this authoritarian state
that has completely

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.790
jeopardized its population's wellbeing

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.410
by shutting down dissent,

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:12.430
by conducting widespread
extra judicial killings.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:15.680
These two stories are mutually compatible.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.550
They are both true.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.110
And I think the, you
know, the reality is that

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:23.170
Rwanda's developmental
progress has been made possible

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:26.090
by its authoritarian nature, right?

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:30.570
Rwanda's success, in terms
of not having another

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.880
outbreak of violence has been possible

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.610
because of its massively robust

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:38.790
military and security apparatus.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.490
Some of the gains women have made

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:44.380
are undermined by the
authoritarian nature of the state.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.240
Disproportionate numbers of wealthy,

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:49.600
foreign-born Tutsi women
sit at the top of the table.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.550
Which means new forms of inequality

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.510
are developing in Rwanda,

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:56.240
impacting the incredible
progress women made

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:59.130
in the immediate
aftermath of the violence.

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:00.450
Rwanda leads the world

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:03.140
when it comes to the
number of women in power,

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:06.960
but it's only one part of the
bigger picture of equality.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.530
For me, the real lesson of Rwanda is that,

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:14.310
getting women into politics
is only one of many

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:17.670
things we must do to actually,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:20.520
you know, increase women's
power and more importantly,

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:24.490
dismantle forms of
oppression against women.

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:26.140
If the level of women in government

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.950
is not matched by a corresponding,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:32.890
kind of strong women's movement
rooted in civil society,

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:34.240
then the women in government

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:36.780
are almost always going to be tokenized.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:40.000
That sort of corresponding
need for women in government

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:44.420
and kind of the
restructuring of the economy

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:48.410
of kind of the kind of patriarchal roots

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:53.410
of the family and of society
and of changing kind of

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.600
the systems of governance as well,

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.680
that's the way that we build

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:00.690
a more gender equal future.

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.060
This process we are going
through as a country,

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.740
I call it a rebirth.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:06.360
I must say that Rwanda,

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.090
when you compare it to
other African countries,

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:12.100
we are definitely fortunate
because we have a system,

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:15.890
a system that responds
to the needs of women.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.130
So we keep reminding ourselves

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:21.000
that we worked for it and we deserve it.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:22.820
We are now saying we need to raise

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:25.850
our sons and daughters differently.

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:28.190
We've come to understand

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.240
because we have enjoyed
the new opportunities

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.370
that the new Rwanda offers,

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:37.290
and doing what is undoable, it's tough.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.770
Let's continue but at the same time,

00:17:38.770 --> 00:17:41.630
make sure that the generation to come

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:44.476
sees things differently.

